"""Visuals and explicit prerequisite examples for the PowerPoint teaching route."""
def enrich(slides, week):
    def prose(title, body, notes=''):
        return dict(title=title, body=body, notes=notes)
    def code(title, program, caption, expected):
        return dict(title=title, body='', kind='code', code=program, caption=caption,
                    notes='Read each symbol before running. Ask everyone to predict. Expected output: '+expected)
    def table(title, rows, caption):
        return dict(title=title, body='', kind='table', table=rows, caption=caption, notes=caption)
    def figure(title, name, caption, notes):
        return dict(title=title, body='', kind='figure', image='figs/'+name+'.png', caption=caption,
                    notes=notes+'\nSource: existing course illustration figs/'+name+'.png. These are illustrative examples, not this cohort’s assessed results.')
    additions={
      2:[figure('Cells share one Python session','notebook_anatomy','A code cell runs instructions. A text cell explains them.', 'Point to the code, saved output and shared memory. The pictured filename is illustrative. Open proof_of_life.ipynb for today’s work.'),
         figure('Execution order changes the result','execution_trap','Restart, then run all cells in order before submission.','Trace 12 + 5 = 17, then 22, then 27. Restarting clears memory. Running the setup cell restores 12.')],
      3:[table('Python values have types', [['Type','Example','Meaning'],['int','3','Whole number'],['float','3.5','Decimal approximation'],['str','"3.5"','Text in quotes'],['bool','True / False','Logical value'],['NoneType','None','No value supplied']], '3.5 + 2 gives 5.5. "3.5" + "2" joins text into "3.52".'),
         code('Names, conversion and comments','length_m = float("3.5")  # text to number\nwidth_m = 2\narea_m2 = length_m * width_m\nprint(area_m2)\nprint(type(length_m))','type(...) reports the kind of value. <class \'float\'> means a floating-point number.','7.0 then <class \'float\'>'),
         figure('A variable refers to its current value','variable_box','After reassignment, thickness refers to 0.20.','This box is a simplified model of a name referring to a value. Other names could still refer to an earlier object.'),
         code('A decision needs a colon and indentation','temperature = 28\nif temperature >= 30:\n    print("warm")\nelse:\n    print("below 30")','>= compares values. Only the chosen indented block runs.','below 30. Change 28 to 30 and predict warm. This is a toy threshold, not an engineering limit.'),
         figure('Inputs, instructions and a returned result','function_anatomy','The function body runs when we call the function.','Read def, the name, parameters, colon, indentation, return and call. Definition creates the function. Its body waits for a call. Distinguish print, which displays, from return, which passes a value back.'),
         code('Positional and named inputs','def rectangle_area(length, width):\n    return length * width\n\nprint(rectangle_area(3, 2))\nprint(rectangle_area(width=2, length=3))','Both calls supply the same inputs. return makes the result reusable.','6 then 6'),
         prose('Arithmetic that needs care','(2 + 4) / 2 gives 3.0. 2 + 4 / 2 gives 4.0.\n\n7 / 2 gives 3.5. 7 // 2 gives 3. -7 // 2 gives -4.\n\nFloating-point numbers approximate many decimals. Compare calculated measurements with a stated tolerance.','Floor division rounds down. Use parentheses to state the intended calculation. Keep units in variable names.')],
      4:[figure('A loop changes the running total','accumulator_trace','Follow one reading through the code and the trace table.','The displayed mean is rounded to two decimals. Keep full precision in calculations. This larger illustration follows the same rule as our smaller 2, 4, 6 example.'),
         code('A list stores items in order','readings = [2, 4, 6]\nprint(readings[0])\nreadings.append(8)\nprint(len(readings))\nprint(readings[1:3])','[] builds a list. Positions start at zero. The slice stops before position 3.','2 then 4 then [4, 6]. readings[4] would raise IndexError.'),
         code('A dictionary gives each value a key','record = {"unit": "A", "reading": 4}\nprint(record["reading"])\nrecord["reading"] = 6\nprint(record.get("missing", 0))\nfor key, value in record.items():\n    print(key, value)','{} builds a dictionary. Keys identify values. items() supplies key/value pairs.','4, then 0, then unit A and reading 6. Dictionaries preserve insertion order, but access by key is the important distinction. An unknown key with [] raises KeyError.'),
         table('Three ways to collect values',[['Collection','Example','Use'],['list','[2, 4, 6]','Ordered items you may change'],['tuple','(2, 4)','Fixed collection'],['set','{2, 4}','Unique values / membership']], 'A dictionary uses key: value pairs, such as {"reading": 4}.'),
         prose('A loop repeats an indented block','for reading in readings: takes one item at a time.\n\nThe colon starts the block. Four spaces show which lines repeat.\n\nA running total must start at zero before the loop.','Trace the native loop table that follows. Predict total after each item. An empty list needs a guard before dividing by its length.')],
      5:[figure('A labelled table in Python','dataframe_anatomy','A row is an observation. A column is a named field.','Point to one row, column, index and dtype. This maintenance table is illustrative. Work with the issued cohort table next.'),
         figure('A comparison creates a Boolean mask','boolean_mask','True keeps a row. False excludes it from this selection.','Read > as greater than, then trace one row through the mask. Selection does not delete the source rows. 40 is a practice threshold, not a safety standard.'),
         prose('Reading pandas syntax','import pandas as pd loads the library and gives it a short name.\n\npd.read_csv("file.csv") calls a function. The quoted text is the file path.\n\ndf["kwh"] selects a column. df["kwh"].mean() calls a method on that column.','Replace file.csv with the actual issued filename. Explain library, alias, function and method before running the worked example.')],
      6:[figure('Choosing a chart for the question','chart_chooser','Read axis names and units before interpreting a pattern.','Name the question answered by each chart. Correlation does not establish causation. A bar chart usually needs a zero baseline.'),
         code('Copies and duplicate checks','row = {"id": "A", "reading": 4}\ncopy = dict(row)\ncopy["reading"] = 5\nseen = set()\nseen.add(row["id"])\nprint(row["reading"])\nprint("A" in seen)','dict(row) makes a shallow copy. A set keeps unique IDs.','4 then True. The example has only scalar fields. A shallow copy does not independently copy nested lists.'),
         table('Mean, median and unusual readings',[['Readings','Mean','Median'],['2, 4, 6','4','4'],['2, 4, 30','12','4']], 'The mean uses every value. The median is the middle sorted value. A large value needs context.'),
         code('A compact loop is optional','readings = [2, 4, 6]\ndoubled = []\nfor value in readings:\n    doubled.append(value * 2)\nprint(doubled)\n# Same operation in compact form:\nprint([value * 2 for value in readings])','A list comprehension makes the same list. Learn the ordinary loop first.','[4, 8, 12] twice')],
      8:[figure('Fitting a line to observations','fit_by_eye','The fitted line minimises the sum of squared vertical errors.','These are illustrative concrete observations. Point to x, y, one prediction and one residual. A unique slope requires variation in x. A low training error alone does not show generalisation.'),
         code('Rows, columns and named options','import numpy as np\nX = np.array([[1], [2], [3]])\ny = np.array([12, 14, 16])\nprint(X.shape)\nprint(y.shape)','X has three rows and one feature column. y has three target values.','(3, 1) then (3,). Explain nested brackets before model.fit(X, y). Keyword arguments such as random_state=42 name an option.'),
         table('A prediction error in ordinary numbers',[['Actual','Prediction','Absolute error'],['10','8','2'],['20','24','4']], 'MAE = (2 + 4) / 2 = 3, in the target’s unit. Compare a model against a training-only baseline.')],
      9:[table('A category can become indicator columns',[['Material','is_steel','is_timber'],['steel','1','0'],['timber','0','1']], 'An indicator is 1 when its category applies and 0 otherwise. Fit the encoder on training data.'),
         prose('Probability and a warning decision','A predicted probability of 0.7 means an estimated 70% chance of class 1.\n\nWith a threshold of 0.5, 0.7 produces warning 1.\n\nA probability is an estimate. It needs evaluation and may be poorly calibrated.','Lowering a threshold on fixed predictions cannot reduce the number flagged. Precision may rise or fall. Evaluate costs using validation data.')],
      10:[figure('Each fold takes a turn at validation','kfold','Illustrated spread: population SD 0.39 MPa, not a confidence interval.','The illustrated mean is about 4.30 MPa and population standard deviation about 0.39 MPa. This is fold variation, not a confidence interval. Keep equipment groups together. Keep a separate final test outside model selection.'),
          prose('What standard deviation tells us','Scores 3, 4 and 5 have mean 4.\n\nTheir deviations from the mean are -1, 0 and 1.\n\nThe sample standard deviation is sqrt((1 + 0 + 1) / 2) = 1.','Explain square root as undoing a square. The sample denominator is n - 1. This describes spread, not a guarantee about future error.')],
      11:[table('An interaction uses two known inputs',[['Current A','Resistance ohm','I squared × R W'],['2','3','12'],['4','3','48']], 'A derived input must be available when predicting. A useful physical relationship still needs validation.')],
      12:[prose('A model comparison needs a fair test','Use the same validation observations for every candidate.\n\nFit preprocessing on training data only.\n\nChoose the error measure and practical requirements before inspecting results.','A tree asks successive questions. A forest combines many trees. More complexity does not guarantee better validation performance. The task’s tie-break rule remains authoritative.')],
      13:[figure('A reproducible notebook starts fresh','execution_trap','The saved outputs must agree with a fresh run in the stated order.','Demonstrate restarting, uploading the issued data, and running all. The same result also depends on data, package versions and any random seeds.')]
    }.get(week, [])
    # Place explicit prerequisites before terminology and the first assessed interface.
    slides[3:3] = additions
    for s in slides:
        s['notes'] = s.get('notes','') + '\nTeaching rhythm: explain the picture or syntax, predict, run, then change one input. Do not advance until learners can explain the result. Prioritise required task prerequisites over optional extensions.'
